#chap-header("3", [Scene 2: The Dump Site], "Where the bodies are left — and rise")

*Time budget: 65 minutes, most of it combat. Lesson: initiative, AP,
attacks and damage, conditions, and terrain.*

The ravine is where Šauhahten disposes of his victims after extracting
their souls; the shallow cave at its head is where the extractions
happen. Both the evidence and the fight live here --- the party gets the
full horror of the operation in a single location.

== The Scene

The ravine lies in a fold of the Weald's foothills where a seasonal
stream has cut a channel through soft stone. It runs roughly
northeast-to-southwest, forty feet long and twenty feet across at its
widest, narrowing at both ends. The walls are uneven --- fifteen feet
sheer on the north side, sloping to eight feet of rough grade on the
south. At the ravine's head, a low cave mouth is screened by brush that
has been cut and replaced many times.

#aloud[
  You smell it before you see it. Not rot, exactly --- something drier,
  more clinical. The trail leads to a shallow ravine screened by deadfall
  and brush. Someone has piled cut branches over the opening. It is not a
  natural formation.

  Beneath the screen, you find the bodies.

  There are ten of them, laid out in a rough row along the west wall,
  partially covered by a weathered tarp weighed down with stones. They
  are not decomposed --- they are _desiccated_, as if every drop of
  vitality has been wrung from them. Their skin is paper-thin, stretched
  tight over bone. Their eyes are open, and their expressions are not
  peaceful. They look as though something was pulled from them, and they
  felt every moment of it.

  Six of them wear the colors of Never-Still peacekeepers.
]

=== Terrain Features

The following features matter in the fight. Sketch the ravine before the
session; with seven players you want the map ready, not improvised.

- *Deadfall screen (southwest entry):* Cut branches piled over the
  ravine's opening, where the trail from Keshto arrives. Cover; blocks
  line of sight from outside. Moving through costs 1 extra square. 1 AP
  pulls the screen aside.
- *East slope game trail:* A narrow switchback down the east wall --- the
  route the beasts use. A creature on it has elevation over the floor
  (advantage on ranged attacks downward, disadvantage on melee from
  below). Only one Medium creature per section.
- *Drainage channel:* A muddy channel down the ravine floor. Difficult
  terrain. A character knocked Prone in it is also Slowed (save ends).
- *Rocky outcrops (north ridgeline):* Full cover and a vantage point.
  Climbing up costs 2 squares of movement, or 1 with an *Athletics D2*.
  (Pâhâk's gatling wants to be here.)
- *Body row (west wall):* Difficult terrain --- uneven ground, loose
  stones, the tarp itself. The husks rise from here.
- *The cave (northeast head):* The extraction site (see below). During
  the fight it is simply a dark mouth in the rock; Vazhan rises just
  outside it.

== Combat: The Risen Watch

Not all the bodies are still. The Fog boundary's proximity and the
residue of imperfect soul extraction have gotten into the older corpses.
Šauhahten does not know they have risen, and would not care if he did.

*Trigger:* the husks animate when the party approaches the body row or
begins examining the corpses. If the party is standing near the row, the
husks rise within melee reach --- this is deliberate; the moment should
be shocking. An *Awareness D3* before that notices the tarp moving:
characters who succeed are not surprised in the first round.

*Encounter composition (7 characters):*
- 6 *Soulless Husks* --- the reanimated peacekeepers, rising from the row
- 2 *Fog-Touched Beasts* --- arriving at the top of the game trail at the
  start of round 2 (or present from round 1, crouched in the north-slope
  brush, if the party blundered through their hunting ground in Scene 1)
- *Vazhan, Hollow Revenant* --- rising at the cave mouth at the start of
  round 2

Stat blocks are in the Appendix.

#aloud[
  (At the start of round 2:)

  Something else is standing at the cave mouth. It did not shamble up
  like the others --- it is simply _there_, a halberd in its hands, held
  in perfect drill-ground guard. It wears a sergeant's sash.

  When it opens its mouth, a dozen voices come out at once, and none of
  them are its own.
]

#note[
  *Vazhan.* Šauhahten's extraction failed partway on the sergeant ---
  a fragment of soul stayed in the body, and the Fog wove the spilled
  residue of the other victims around it. What rose is faster, stronger,
  and far angrier than a husk, and it has power the others do not.

  If Ushtâng is in play, this is his scene. Vazhan's face is still
  recognisable. The *What Remains* ability on the stat block gives
  Ushtâng (or anyone who invokes Vazhan's name) a way to reach whatever
  is left --- use it to put roleplay _inside_ the fight, not after it.
  When Vazhan is finally destroyed, the fragment of him comes loose:
  for a heartbeat the voice is singular, and it says Ushtâng's name, and
  then it is gone. That is the closure the contract cannot pay for.
]

#note[
  *Running the fight (7 players):*
  - The husks share one initiative count, the beasts another, Vazhan his
    own. Three enemy turns per round, no more.
  - The husks are mindless: nearest living creature, Slam, attempt to
    Grab. They do not coordinate or retreat. Let new players learn the
    rhythm of attack rolls against things that die in one or two hits.
  - The beasts are predators, not tacticians: Pounce from the game trail
    onto whoever is isolated or Grabbed. A beast below half HP flees up
    the trail and into the Weald.
  - Vazhan alternates: Wail of the Taken when two or more characters are
    within its Burst 2, otherwise Halberd against the biggest threat in
    reach, Grasp of Residue against ranged attackers he cannot reach.
    He fights like the sergeant he was --- he holds the cave mouth and
    does not chase.
  - *Terrain matters, say so:* Grabbed in the channel means Slowed too;
    the ridgeline rewards climbers; the game trail funnels the beasts.

  *Dial:* if the party is cruising, the Wail is available every round; if
  they are struggling, Vazhan spends a round "remembering" after any
  character invokes his name.
]

*XP:* 6 Husks (42) + 2 Beasts (30) + Vazhan (30) = *102*.

#attention[
  *Narrative beat:* the party is fighting the people they were sent to
  find. The missing peacekeepers from the reeve's list are the husks
  wearing watch colors. Let the moment land before initiative --- and
  let the fight be fun anyway. Grief and glee coexist at RPG tables;
  that is normal.
]

== After the Fight: The Cave

The cave at the ravine's head is where the extractions happen --- a rough
chamber perhaps fifteen feet across, floor swept clean.

#aloud[
  Concentric circles have been carved into the stone floor, filled with a
  residue that catches the light --- pale, faintly luminescent. At the
  centre, iron stakes have been driven into the rock, fitted with
  restraints. There are old stains on the restraints that do not need
  explanation.

  Along the walls, crude shelves hold alchemical apparatus: glass
  vessels, copper tubing, a distillation rig too heavy to move easily.
  Some vessels still hold a pale, oily substance that seems to move when
  you are not looking directly at it. The Fog curls into the cave and
  pools around the circles, as if drawn to them.

  This place has been used many times.
]

=== Investigating the Scene

Give every player a chance to poke at something. The checks below reward
different sheets; none of them gates progress.

- *Occultism D3:* The bodies and the circles agree: souls have been
  _extracted_ --- not killed out of, but drawn out of, these people, by
  deliberate means. This is not a predator and not a natural Fog
  phenomenon. Someone did this with knowledge and intention.
- *Medicine D4 or Alchemy D4:* No wounds, no poison, no disease. The
  desiccation is a side effect: the body collapses inward once the
  animating principle is removed. Clean, almost surgical, performed many
  times. (Wishanya reads this fluently --- "methodical means a
  practitioner," exactly as she suspected.)
- *Investigate D3:* The site has been used for months. The scoring shows
  layered circles, each slightly refined from the last --- the
  perpetrator is _iterating_. The volume matches the missing list.
  Single set of footprints, coming and going, southwest each time.
- *Ritual D3 or Occultism D4:* This is not necromancy. Necromancy
  manipulates the boundary between life and death; this treats the soul
  as a _substance to be distilled_. Scholarly, disciplined --- the work of
  a trained order, not a hedge-cultist. (Kho Prea's Ritual 6 shines.)

*Found without a check, tucked behind the distillation rig:* a discarded
notebook page in a precise scholarly hand --- calculations, a sketch of a
circle revision, and a sour marginal note: _"The Order's foundations were
correct. Their courage failed, not their theory."_

#note[
  The notebook page is the safety net: whatever the party rolls, they
  leave this scene knowing the method is the work of a scholarly order
  --- which is the thread Merzaghaal pulls on when they return. The
  checks above add texture and let specialists feel specialised; the
  page guarantees the plot.

  *What the party should know leaving this scene:* souls are being
  extracted by methodical, scholarly alchemy; the perpetrator was not
  here and does not live here; the trail of evidence points back to a
  learned order. Time to go home and ask better questions.
]
